t of trades  and we've lost out on the guy that we  wanted so it does happen  but this year worked out really really  well for us  is the etiquette to not ask the team  that's trading up into your spot who  they're gonna take because i remember a  couple of years ago when the 49ers and  the bears flip-flop the 49ers didn't  know who the bears were taking it to  when the 49ers went to three and the  bears may have taken their guide is it  does it ever happen where there's a  we'll do this trade as long as we're  clear that you're not taking our guy  i've heard that before i've heard teams  ask that question  you know i think that we haven't done  that  you know in the last however many years  we've just kind of winged it because i  think would you  i mean my mindset is when you make a  decision to trade  you're making an organizational decision  and you're you're on board with that  decision  all bets are off i mean it could be a  situation where team actually tells you  that they're taking somebody and they're  taking somebody else uh we've had  situations in the past where teams  haven't always been honest with us in  various situations  so you know i haven't asked that  question i suppose i could have asked  howie roseman that question he might  have given me the answer but you know he  could have also been on the clock and  traded i mean he might have gotten a 22  and somebody else comes along and says  hey we want that pick i mean our phone  was ringing multiple teams were calling  us  so you know i think once you make that  decision to make the trade once you make  that decision to trade away from the  pick  you're not in control anymore you take  the picture gonna get and you move on  what's the biggest challenge of drafting  a guy like marquis brown who had the  foot injury was able to do nothing  pre-draft  you're relying on his tape you can't do  the apples to apples comparisons what's  the biggest challenge of of feeling good  about still making that pick in round  one  probably that i mean you just basically  answered your own question i mean a guy  hasn't worked out he hasn't run a 40.  you've got to go strictly off the tape  you've got to go strictly off what  people say about him  and fortunately we've always based our  evaluations of players on their tape  first and foremost so we love this tape  we thought he was an explosive player  dynamic college football player whose  skill set really did fit our team and  what we're trying to be as an offense  and so there's always a little bit of  apprehension anytime a guy doesn't work  out it seems like more and more nowadays  players aren't working out quite as much  so we go back to the tape we trust our  people we get multiple looks we talk to  people at school and we make our  assessment  the other receiver you drafted this guy  was the 30th pick in round three miles  boy can you ran a 4-4-2 a couple of fast  receivers how much of that speed desire  is influenced by  the changing quarterback the type of  offense you're running now and just any  other adjustment that's happened as you  go from joe flacco to lamar jackson  well i think lamar factors in we want to  put weapons around him his skill set  being probably the fastest player on the  field in most games  it makes it tougher for teams to  basically you know  basically all focus on him he doesn't  have quite the bull's-eye maybe on his  chest if these guys end up being  legitimate players  i think in general mike you know we want  to be the fastest team we can football  has changed  you know back in 2009 2010 we used to  play the steelers and those games were  like you know blood and guts smash mouth  football  and football has changed  it's changed a lot we play these dynamic  offenses we play the steelers twice a  year we play the bengals uh with the  speed that they have on offense we  played the chiefs this year we played  the saints this year  you know we've played the rams we  scrimmaged against the rams 